{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_019MvQgSog4RyfX6cUx2yf2s","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants me to generate detailed predicted data for every \"planned\" experiment in the experiment_plan, under the \"worst\" tier scenario. Let me identify all planned experiments:\n\n- exp-004: MAVEN confusability study (status: planned in the plan, but there's a registry entry exp004_maven_bcs that is DONE with conclusion: negative, and exp-004 DONE with conclusion: success for SCS computation)\n- exp-005: DocRED confusability study (planned)\n- exp-006: Interaction effect 2x2 (planned)\n\n\n- exp-007: Error taxonomy (planned)\n- exp-012: Multi-model validation with LLaMA (planned)\n- exp-008: SDGD-zero on Few-NERD (there's a registry entry showing DONE/failed and a RUNNING r2)\n- exp-009: SDGD-zero on MAVEN (planned)\n- exp-010: SDGD-cal on Few-NERD (planned)\n- exp-011: SDGD-cal on MAVEN (planned)\n- exp-013: Ablation random batching (planned)\n- exp-014: Ablation per-type splitting (planned)\n- exp-015: SCS validation (planned)\n\nI need to double-check the experiment plan to make sure I'm capturing all the planned experiments correctly. Looking at the rest of the planned experiments, I need to generate worst-tier predictions for all of them. Let me recall what defines the worst tier: SDGD completely fails with F1 scores below single-pass baselines and drops of 3 percentage points or more, shows no significant difference from random, has limited cross-model and cross-task generalization except for Qwen3×Few-NERD, and produces non-significant or inconsistent pairwise signals for LLaMA.\n\nFor the worst tier specifically, I'm looking at metric ranges where SDGD delta against single-pass falls between -8 and -3 percentage points, delta against random stays between -3 and 1 percentage points, LLaMA pairwise correlations hover around -0.02 to 0.07 with p-values between 0.05 and 0.8, FewRel formal deltas stay modest at 3-12 percentage points, and only 1-2 cross-task pairwise comparisons show significance. The narrative here shifts from claiming SDGD's effectiveness to examining what we actually learned about schema complexity in LLM information extraction.\n\nNow I'm working through the MAVEN confusability experiment in detail—this one already has partial registry data, so I need to map out what worst-case results would look like. The count curve might show F1 increasing with entity count rather than decreasing as confusability would predict, or staying flat. The pairwise confusion scores would only show weak signals in MiniLM while Qwen fails entirely. Per-type BCS could flip positive due to centrality confounds, and within-count correlations wouldn't reach significance.\n\nFor DocRED with its 96 relation types at document level, the worst outcome is complete signal loss—BCS correlation either flat or inverted, with document-level complexity drowning out any type-specific confusability effects. The interaction experiment would show no significant interaction term despite a visible count effect, with ANOVA p-values above 0.1. And in the error taxonomy analysis, the type substitution ratio between high and low BCS bins would compress down to just 3-10x rather than the 10-28x range I'd see in extreme cases.\n\nThe directional pattern holds but becomes noisy with random batching. When I test with LLaMA instead of Qwen3, the pairwise correlation drops below 0.06 with p>0.05, and per-type effects disappear entirely—suggesting the confusability signal is model-dependent. The SDGD-zero approach on Few-NERD already failed once and is running again, but the worst case would be F1 scores 3-8 percentage points below single-pass, since graph coloring batches lose cross-type context and confusability minimization can't recover that. MAVEN performs even worse under SDGD-zero due to its homogeneity, and calibrated SDGD on Few-NERD only marginally improves over standard SD.\n\nWhen I look at the ablations, random batching performs nearly identically to SDGD (within -3 to 1 F1 points), which means confusability-aware grouping provides no real benefit. Per-type splitting actually outperforms everything, indicating batch count matters far more than composition. The embedding-based SCS validation also shows weak correlation with the empirical confusion matrix (ρ < 0.15), so the embedding proxy isn't reliable for predicting confusability.\n\nNow I'm compiling the detailed metrics for each experiment and thinking through what tables and figures would best illustrate these findings. For exp-004 specifically, I need to check whether the registry already contains the pairwise analysis data—MiniLM shows a weak but consistent cross-task signal (r≈0.120), Qwen3 fails entirely (r≈-0.007), and the per-type and count-based approaches reveal confounds and unusual patterns in MAVEN's structure. Let me verify the current status and pull together the full results.\n\nI'm going to write out the comprehensive YAML now with all the metrics, rationales, and figure descriptions. For the narrative framing in the worst-case scenario, I should completely drop the SDGD angle and reposition this as a pure empirical analysis—moving from research question through controlled experiments to findings and implications. I'll be thorough with tables and figure descriptions for each experiment. Looking at the existing MAVEN results, the exact match F1 scores are extremely low (0.003-0.035), making trigger boundary matching essentially infeasible, while relaxed F1 is more usable at 0.089-0.247. Interestingly, the high BCS batches show higher relaxed F1 than low BCS ones, which suggests the centrality confound is working in the opposite direction at the batch level. For the worst tier prediction, the full pairwise analysis would likely show MiniLM with a weak correlation (r≈0.120) but limited practical impact given how poor the exact F1 is, while Qwen3 probably won't show significance. For exp-008, since it already failed once, the second run is unlikely to recover.\n\nNow I'm laying out the realistic numbers for both experiments in structured format.\n\nFor exp-004 with MAVEN, the pairwise Qwen3 correlation isn't significant, while the per-type correlations hold steady. The within-count BCS-F1 relationships are weak and not statistically significant, and the confusion rate ratios are more moderate than what we saw with Few-NERD. I should note that for the worst tier analysis, I'm predicting what the full study with proper sampling would reveal rather than relying on partial existing data.\n\nMoving to exp-005 with DocRED's 96 relation types at the document level, I'm starting to think through what patterns might emerge in the worst-performing tier there. The BCS effect is weak. Looking at the error taxonomy across different bins, type substitution shows only modest increases in high-BCS cases, while boundary errors and false negatives don't vary by BCS at all—so the confusability channel is only partially supported. Hallucination rates also don't differ by BCS. When I test this on LLaMA-3.3-70B across Few-NERD, the correlation is negligible and not statistically significant, with confusion ratios ranging from 2-4x.\n\nNow examining the batch processing experiments: SDGD-zero on Few-NERD drops F1 by 4-8 percentage points compared to single-pass because merging batches loses cross-type context, causing entities in overlapping sentences to get processed multiple times with different type subsets and producing inconsistent predictions. The same approach on MAVEN performs even worse, dropping F1 by about 5 percentage points.\n\nWhen I calibrate the batching strategy with a few examples, it captures empirical confusion patterns better than the embedding-based approach, but the fundamental issue of context loss during decomposition persists—SDGD-cal still underperforms single-pass by 2-4 percentage points across both datasets. Interestingly, random batching with the same number of batches performs comparably to SDGD, suggesting the graph coloring strategy isn't providing much advantage, likely because MAVEN's highly homogeneous types force the algorithm to create many batches anyway, which increases both latency and boundary inconsistencies.\n\nLooking at per-type splitting, it's actually the worst approach—forcing one type per batch causes such severe context loss that performance drops to 0.323, well below even random batching at 0.375 and SDGD at 0.37, making it clear that extreme decomposition hurts more than it helps.\n\nThe SCS validation shows only weak correlation with empirical confusion (ρ=0.12, p=0.04), which explains why the zero-shot approach failed to improve performance.\n\nNow I'm ready to compile the comprehensive YAML summary and think through what tables and visualizations would best present these experimental results.\n\nI need to be careful distinguishing between what the existing MAVEN data already demonstrates versus what I'm predicting for the worst tier—the registry shows some concerning patterns in the full event detection study that I should account for.\n\nNow I'm organizing the output as a comprehensive YAML structure with clear expected results and detailed rationales, using markdown tables embedded as list items to match the template format.\n\nGenerating the worst-tier scenario... I'm looking at the V2 prompt baseline for Few-NERD to ground my predictions—the single-pass performance drops to F1=0.306-0.347 when batch sizes shrink to 6-11 types, which is what SDGD would create by decomposing the 66 entity types across k batches. The key question is whether merging predictions across batches helps or hurts overall performance.\n\nIn the worst case, merge conflicts would drag down the F1 to around 0.364, which is about 5 percentage points below the single-pass baseline of 0.414. That's actually within the expected degradation range for this approach. Now I'm thinking through what happens with random batching in exp-013—if I randomly split the types into the same number of batches and merge, the performance dynamics would be different from a confusability-aware decomposition. The pattern is clear: decomposition consistently hurts performance because smaller batches lose cross-type context, and confusability-aware grouping doesn't outperform random batching. Per-type batching is the worst due to extreme fragmentation. For the FewRel formal experiment, the effect size shrinks to 3-12pp with larger sample sizes and better embeddings, making it statistically insignificant. Now I need to compile all this into the YAML output.\n\nLooking at the explicit experiments in the plan, I should focus on those rather than hypothetical formal studies. For MAVEN specifically, I'm considering the SDGD approach with 168 types—graph coloring on that many types would likely produce 15-25 batches, which becomes computationally expensive to run through multiple passes.\n\nIn the worst-case scenario for SDGD-zero, the high inter-type similarity creates many batches of 7-11 types each, and when merging these batches together, there are significant conflicts. This results in a relaxed F1 around 0.195 compared to 0.245 for a single pass—roughly a 5 percentage point drop.\n\nFor the pairwise analysis on MAVEN, I'm looking at the existing correlations from the paper framing work: MiniLM shows r=0.120 and Qwen3 shows r=-0.007. If I were to run a full 500-sample re-evaluation, the MiniLM correlation might shift slightly given the larger sample size, though the estimate is already based on over 11,000 pairs so movement would likely be modest.\n\nThe key constraint from the worst-tier analysis is that only 1-2 task×embedding combinations should show significant pairwise signal. I already have Qwen3×Few-NERD at r=0.156 and MiniLM×MAVEN at r=0.120, which accounts for both slots, so I need to be careful about what else I claim as significant. I'm mapping out the full MAVEN analysis for exp-004: the count curve and BCS-controlled batches are partially done, the pairwise confusion analysis and per-type breakdowns are computed, but I still need to check the within-count correlation and error taxonomy. Looking at the worst-case scenario for those new components, the correlation doesn't reach significance and the confusion ratios are more modest than Few-NERD's, which actually fits the narrative. Now I need to clarify what \"result_rationale\" means for each finding before writing this up.\n\nFor MAVEN, the 168 event types are all semantically similar since they come from FrameNet, which compresses the BCS range and weakens the signal-to-noise ratio. The centrality confound is also stronger because central types are well-represented in training, and exact trigger matching is inherently noisy for LLMs. For DocRED, the document-level reasoning required—entity coreference, multi-sentence inference—overshadows type confusability effects, and since the 96 relation types span diverse semantic domains, the BCS variation is larger but the confusability signal gets buried.\n\nThe 2x2 design in exp-006 has limited statistical power with only 4 cells and ~5 batches each, so detecting an interaction requires larger effect sizes than what's actually present. The count effect is robust, but the BCS effect is marginal and harder to isolate. For exp-007, error taxonomy classification is inherently noisy—type substitution is the clearest confusability signal but represents only a small fraction of errors, with boundary errors and false negatives dominating instead. Random batches show lower ratios because they have more moderate BCS differences compared to extreme batches.\n\nWith LLaMA-3.3-70B, the embedding geometry and attention patterns differ from Qwen3, so its internal type representations might be more or less separable, potentially affecting how well it handles confusable types.\n\nFor exp-008, graph coloring reduces within-batch confusability but fails to address cross-batch entity overlap where the same entity appears across batches with different type subsets, the loss of cross-type context that would normally provide negative examples, and merge conflicts when different batches predict conflicting types for the same span. These structural issues outweigh the confusability benefit.\n\nNow I'm organizing the rationales for each experiment and preparing to write out the full YAML output.\n\nLooking at the worst-tier predictions across experiments: exp-004 shows that only the MiniLM pairwise correlation holds up (r=0.108, p<0.001), while the Qwen3 pairwise and per-type metrics fail or are confounded by centrality effects, and the confusion rate ratios are extreme at 4-7x. For exp-005, the count curve shows flat F1 performance across the range without a clear trend.\n\nNow examining exp-006's interaction model, the count main effect is significant (F=7.8, p=0.008) but the BCS effect is only marginal (F=2.9, p=0.09), and the interaction itself is negligible (F=0.3, p=0.58), suggesting the effects are mostly additive rather than interactive. In exp-007, the error taxonomy reveals a stark 3.5x substitution ratio between high and low BCS groups compared to 1.4x for random baseline, though boundary and false negative errors show minimal BCS effects.\n\nMoving through the multi-model experiments, exp-012 with LLaMA shows weak correlations (r=0.04, p=0.07 for pairwise; ρ=-0.08, p=0.54 per-type) but a notable 2.5x confusion rate ratio on extreme cases. For the SDGD-zero experiments, exp-008 on Few-NERD reveals that the graph coloring approach with k=8 batches actually hurts performance—F1 drops 5 percentage points compared to single-pass inference, with recall slightly improving but precision substantially degrading, and about 12% of predictions show cross-batch disagreements that create merge conflicts.\n\nThe SDGD-zero approach on MAVEN (exp-009) is even worse, requiring 18 batches due to the dense confusability graph and incurring a steep 5-point F1 penalty along with 18x the API cost. When I move to the calibrated variants, SDGD-cal on Few-NERD (exp-010) narrows the gap to 2.6 points below single-pass, suggesting calibration helps but can't fully compensate for the decomposition overhead. The same pattern holds for SDGD-cal on MAVEN (exp-011), with a 2.7-point drop.\n\nTesting random batching (exp-013) shows almost no difference from confusability-aware grouping—just 0.4 points with no statistical significance—which means the careful graph-based decomposition isn't actually providing a benefit. Per-type splitting (exp-014) performs worst across the board, confirming that fragmenting the problem into many small subproblems is fundamentally harmful. Finally, when I validate the SCS proxy against empirical confusion matrices (exp-015), the correlation is weak and noisy, which explains why SDGD-zero fails so badly—the proxy signal is too unreliable to guide practical decomposition decisions.\n\nNow I'm organizing the results into tables and figures: main metrics across all conditions, statistical significance tests, scatter plots showing correlations, bar charts comparing F1 scores, and confusion matrices as heatmaps. I need to cross-check that baseline numbers stay consistent across experiments—SDGD F1=0.364 should match everywhere it appears, and the MAVEN baselines should align with the original runs. Let me finalize the YAML output with careful attention to numerical consistency. Looking at how SDGD merges results across batches—each type gets assigned to exactly one batch, so there shouldn't be conflicts for the same type, but I need to think through what could go wrong: individual batches might have lower precision on their assigned types, or there could be boundary cases where entities span multiple types. same text span predicted as type A in batch 1 and type B in batch 2 (if different batches see the same text and assign it different types)\n2. Actually, this is unlikely because each batch only extracts its assigned types\n\nWait, but the model might extract entities of types NOT in its assigned batch. The model is instructed to extract only certain types, but it might hallucinate or misinterpret. In practice:\n- If the prompt says \"extract entities of types A, B, C\", the model should only output A, B, C labels\n- But the model might still miss entities or extract wrong types\n\nIn worst tier, the issue is that:\n1. Each batch sees the text with only a subset of types → the model might label an entity as the closest available type rather than its true type (which is in a different batch)\n2. E.g., if \"person-politician\" and \"person-other\" are in different batches, batch 1 with \"person-politician\" might label \"person-other\" entities as \"person-politician\" and vice versa\n\nThis is actually a realistic failure mode for SDGD. By separating confusable types into different batches, the model never sees both options simultaneously, so instead of choosing between them, it just defaults to whichever type is available in its batch. This forces misclassifications and increases type confusion rather than reducing it. The model can't do comparative disambiguation when it only sees one of a confusable pair, which explains why F1 drops—single-pass extraction with all 66 types visible lets the model disambiguate properly, while SDGD's batching approach actually makes this worse.\n\nNow I'm looking at the revised performance numbers to understand the ranking. Random batching actually outperforms SDGD because it accidentally puts some confusable pairs together, allowing occasional disambiguation, whereas SDGD deliberately separates them. The delta comparison confirms this—SDGD performs worse than random on average.\n\nFor calibration, I'm realizing there's a tension: if SDGD-cal uses empirical confusion from the calibration set to build its confusability graph, it would still separate types that show high confusion, which seems counterintuitive since separation is what's causing the problem in the first place.\n\nThe actual improvement likely comes from the empirical graph being sparser than the embedding-based one—fewer edges means less aggressive separation overall, which partially mitigates the harm.\n\nNow I'm working through the specific numbers. The metric ranges constrain SDGD performance to roughly 0.334-0.384 against the single-pass baseline of 0.414, and within ±3 points of random performance. Let me settle on values that respect these bounds while showing SDGD-cal slightly outperforming SDGD-zero but both falling well short of single-pass.\n\nI'm ready to generate the YAML configuration now. Before I do, I need to verify the MAVEN baseline numbers—single-pass relaxed F1 is around 0.245, so SDGD-zero should land roughly 5 percentage points lower.\n\nLooking back at the experiment plan, exp-004 already has the count curve data from the completed MAVEN BCS run, so I should focus the remaining work on the full pairwise and per-type analyses with proper sample sizing.\n\nNow I'm ready to write out the YAML structure for the predictions, organizing each experiment with its name, plan ID, description, expected metrics, rationale for worst-tier results, any markdown tables, figure descriptions, and how the analysis connects to the broader study. the worst tier narrative)\n\nOne thought on FewRel: the experiment plan doesn't have a specific FewRel formal experiment listed as planned. The fewrel_pilot is already DONE in the registry. So I won't include FewRel formal as a separate experiment since it's not in the planned list. But the worst tier does reference fewrel_formal_extreme_delta_pp. I think this refers to a potential extension of the pilot with more samples and formal testing. But since it's not in the experiment plan, I won't include it.\n\nWait, let me re-read the experiment list... No, there's no separate FewRel formal experiment. The FewRel pilot is done. The worst tier metrics for FewRel might refer to what happens when the pilot results are formally tested with more samples, but this isn't a separate planned experiment.\n\nOK, final plan: I'll produce predictions for all 12 planned experiments. Let me map them out: MAVEN full study, DocRED study, the 2x2 interaction experiment, error taxonomy work, the two SDGD-zero variants across Few-NERD and MAVEN, the two SDGD-cal variants, LLaMA multi-model comparison, random batching ablation, per-type splitting, and SCS validation. That's my complete list to work through.\n\nI'll generate the YAML output now.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":17896,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":17896},"output_tokens":21,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d6066cd7-3b7c-4597-bdab-f4c39ace5902","uuid":"4fc55577-87dd-4f0f-97fe-31bf057bb1d9"}
