{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016EwUSDFT85k955QUthjEvv","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — schema-dag-extraction\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (ARR May 25 cycle)\n5\t- Commitment deadline: August 2, 2026\n6\t- Fallback: EMNLP 2026 Findings (same ARR cycle, commitment August 26)\n7\t- Deadline: ARR May 25, 2026 (10 days from today)\n8\t\n9\t## Core Claims (3 conditions)\n10\t\n11\t1. **Confusability Independence**: Type confusability independently predicts extraction quality degradation in training-free LLM IE, beyond what type count alone explains. Evidence required: within-count correlation r(metric, BCS) significant after controlling for count, replicated on >=2 datasets and >=2 models.\n12\t\n13\t2. **Multi-Channel Degradation**: Count and confusability degrade extraction through orthogonal channels -- count causes entity omission (recall decline), while confusability causes type confusion errors (correct spans labeled with wrong types). Evidence required: error taxonomy showing differential error types per channel, monotonic trends in separate metrics.\n14\t\n15\t3. **SDGD Effectiveness**: Confusability-aware schema decomposition (SDGD) -- partitioning types into low-confusability batches via graph coloring on the SCS-thresholded confusability graph -- yields significant F1 gains over single-pass and heuristic decomposition on complex schemas (>=30 types). Evidence required: SDGD > single-pass, SDGD > random batching (same k), SDGD >= per-type splitting (with lower latency).\n16\t\n17\t## Story Spine\n18\t\n19\t- **Problem**: Training-free LLM IE degrades severely on complex schemas (20-60 F1 gap vs supervised). All existing decomposition approaches (per-type splitting, similarity-based partitioning, fixed k-step pipelines) operate on unvalidated assumptions about WHAT makes schemas complex for LLMs.\n20\t\n21\t- **Existing approaches & their gaps**: UniNER splits per-type (gains 3.3 F1 but loses cross-type context, no criterion for when to split). LLM-PEE uses similarity as implicit confusability proxy (never validated empirically). ProUIE/KGGen use fixed task-level decomposition (ignores intra-layer schema structure). No prior work isolates confusability from count in training-free LLM IE.\n22\t\n23\t- **Our framing**: We conduct the first controlled empirical study isolating type confusability from type count in training-free LLM IE. We discover a multi-channel degradation model: count drives recall decline (capacity bottleneck) while confusability drives type confusion (disambiguation failure). We then operationalize this into SDGD -- a principled framework for confusability-minimizing schema decomposition.\n24\t\n25\t- **Cross-task validation**: We validate on two complementary IE tasks — NER (Few-NERD, 66 fine-grained types) and Event Detection (MAVEN, 168 FrameNet-based event types). MAVEN's hierarchical type structure with known sibling confusion rates (14.6-19.8%) provides a natural testbed where confusability is independently documented. Cross-task replication (NER + ED) demonstrates confusability-aware decomposition is task-agnostic, not NER-specific.\n26\t\n27\t- **Headline result template**: \"Controlling for type count, batch confusability score (BCS) independently predicts F1 degradation (partial r=0.30, p<0.05), with high-confusability batches showing 10-28x more type confusion errors. SDGD-zero improves F1 by X points over single-pass on Few-NERD (66 types) and Y points on MAVEN (168 types), matching per-type splitting quality at Z% of the API cost.\"\n28\t\n29\t## Core Insight (Narrative A -- Multi-Channel Degradation)\n30\t\n31\tSchema-induced degradation in LLM IE is not a single phenomenon but a multi-channel process:\n32\t- **Channel 1 (Count -> Recall)**: More types in the prompt -> attention dilution -> entities missed. Monotonic, near-linear. Addressable by reducing batch size.\n33\t- **Channel 2 (Confusability -> Type Confusion)**: Semantically similar co-present types -> interference during type assignment -> correct entities labeled with wrong types. Addressable by separating confusable types into different batches.\n34\t\n35\tThese channels are orthogonal and independently addressable. SDGD targets Channel 2; batch size reduction targets Channel 1; combining both yields additive gains.\n36\t\n37\t## Evidence Hierarchy (D008 — restructured after type frequency confound discovery)\n38\t\n39\t**Layer 1 (Primary — strongest)**: Pairwise BCS-confusion analysis\n40\t- Pearson r=0.156 (p=4.6e-12, n=1953 type pairs); nonzero-only Spearman ρ=0.254 (p=1.1e-6)\n41\t- Top confused pairs are all high-BCS semantic neighbors (e.g., person-other ↔ person-politician, BCS=0.934)\n42\t- This analysis is immune to type frequency confound (operates at type-pair level)\n43\t\n44\t**Layer 2 (Supporting — pending)**: Per-type F1 vs mean BCS analysis\n45\t- Controls for type frequency by computing F1 per type, not per batch\n46\t- Partial correlation controlling for type frequency\n47\t- Status: analysis in progress\n48\t\n49\t**Layer 3 (Discussion — transparent reporting)**: Batch-level BCS-F1 positive correlation\n50\t- Within-count batch-level r(F1, BCS) is POSITIVE (high BCS → high F1), opposite to naive prediction\n51\t- Explanation: type frequency confound — high-frequency types tend to have high BCS AND high F1\n52\t- This is NOT evidence for the hypothesis; reported transparently as methodological finding\n53\t- Demonstrates the importance of controlling for confounds — strengthens methodological rigor\n54\t\n55\t**Methodological note**: The positive batch-level correlation motivated our shift from batch-level to type-pair-level and per-type analysis. This progression (naive → controlled → mechanistic) is itself a methodological contribution.\n56\t\n57\t## Venue Fit Risks\n58\t\n59\t- **\"Just an empirical study\"**: Mitigated by SDGD as direct operationalization. Controlled experimental design parallels \"Lost in the Middle\" (TACL 2024) methodology.\n60\t- **Training-free IE relevance**: Cite deployment scenarios -- clinical (mCODEGPT), regulatory (FINRA 2026), multilingual (50+ languages with no IE corpora). Training-free is sometimes the ONLY option.\n61\t- **Multi-model generalizability**: MVP used only Qwen3-32B. Need >=2 models. Plan: add one model from different family.\n62\t- **Over-prediction confound**: F1 curve reversal at low counts needs careful framing. Present recall as clean degradation signal; precision artifact explained in methodology section.\n63\t\n64\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n65\t\n66\t| Concern | Response Strategy | Experiment |\n67\t|---------|------------------|------------|\n68\t| Only one model | Add >=1 more model | exp-012 |\n69\t| Only one dataset for Claim 1 | Replicate on MAVEN (168 event types, cross-task) + optionally DocRED | exp-004, exp-005 |\n70\t| No practical method | SDGD framework | exp-008 to exp-011 |\n71\t| Confusability measure validity | SCS validation: embedding vs empirical | exp-015 |\n72\t| Effect size small | Report batch-level + type-pair-level + error decomposition | exp-006, exp-007 |\n73\t| Why not just per-type splitting? | Ablation: SDGD vs per-type vs random | exp-013, exp-014 |\n74\t| Latency overhead of decomposition | Report API calls and wall-clock time | exp-008 |\n75\t| Type frequency confound | Transparent reporting: batch-level positive correlation + per-type and pairwise analysis controlling for frequency | exp-002, per-type analysis |\n76\t| BCS direction unexpected | Pairwise analysis (immune to frequency) confirms hypothesis; batch-level reported in discussion | exp-002 |\n77\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"d6066cd7-3b7c-4597-bdab-f4c39ace5902","uuid":"dc5e436a-b261-4cce-bea3-8bc2674e06b3","timestamp":"2026-05-15T11:58:03.688Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_18/projects/schema-dag-extraction/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260515T053106/worst/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — schema-dag-extraction\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: EMNLP 2026 Main (ARR May 25 cycle)\n- Commitment deadline: August 2, 2026\n- Fallback: EMNLP 2026 Findings (same ARR cycle, commitment August 26)\n- Deadline: ARR May 25, 2026 (10 days from today)\n\n## Core Claims (3 conditions)\n\n1. **Confusability Independence**: Type confusability independently predicts extraction quality degradation in training-free LLM IE, beyond what type count alone explains. Evidence required: within-count correlation r(metric, BCS) significant after controlling for count, replicated on >=2 datasets and >=2 models.\n\n2. **Multi-Channel Degradation**: Count and confusability degrade extraction through orthogonal channels -- count causes entity omission (recall decline), while confusability causes type confusion errors (correct spans labeled with wrong types). Evidence required: error taxonomy showing differential error types per channel, monotonic trends in separate metrics.\n\n3. **SDGD Effectiveness**: Confusability-aware schema decomposition (SDGD) -- partitioning types into low-confusability batches via graph coloring on the SCS-thresholded confusability graph -- yields significant F1 gains over single-pass and heuristic decomposition on complex schemas (>=30 types). Evidence required: SDGD > single-pass, SDGD > random batching (same k), SDGD >= per-type splitting (with lower latency).\n\n## Story Spine\n\n- **Problem**: Training-free LLM IE degrades severely on complex schemas (20-60 F1 gap vs supervised). All existing decomposition approaches (per-type splitting, similarity-based partitioning, fixed k-step pipelines) operate on unvalidated assumptions about WHAT makes schemas complex for LLMs.\n\n- **Existing approaches & their gaps**: UniNER splits per-type (gains 3.3 F1 but loses cross-type context, no criterion for when to split). LLM-PEE uses similarity as implicit confusability proxy (never validated empirically). ProUIE/KGGen use fixed task-level decomposition (ignores intra-layer schema structure). No prior work isolates confusability from count in training-free LLM IE.\n\n- **Our framing**: We conduct the first controlled empirical study isolating type confusability from type count in training-free LLM IE. We discover a multi-channel degradation model: count drives recall decline (capacity bottleneck) while confusability drives type confusion (disambiguation failure). We then operationalize this into SDGD -- a principled framework for confusability-minimizing schema decomposition.\n\n- **Cross-task validation**: We validate on two complementary IE tasks — NER (Few-NERD, 66 fine-grained types) and Event Detection (MAVEN, 168 FrameNet-based event types). MAVEN's hierarchical type structure with known sibling confusion rates (14.6-19.8%) provides a natural testbed where confusability is independently documented. Cross-task replication (NER + ED) demonstrates confusability-aware decomposition is task-agnostic, not NER-specific.\n\n- **Headline result template**: \"Controlling for type count, batch confusability score (BCS) independently predicts F1 degradation (partial r=0.30, p<0.05), with high-confusability batches showing 10-28x more type confusion errors. SDGD-zero improves F1 by X points over single-pass on Few-NERD (66 types) and Y points on MAVEN (168 types), matching per-type splitting quality at Z% of the API cost.\"\n\n## Core Insight (Narrative A -- Multi-Channel Degradation)\n\nSchema-induced degradation in LLM IE is not a single phenomenon but a multi-channel process:\n- **Channel 1 (Count -> Recall)**: More types in the prompt -> attention dilution -> entities missed. Monotonic, near-linear. Addressable by reducing batch size.\n- **Channel 2 (Confusability -> Type Confusion)**: Semantically similar co-present types -> interference during type assignment -> correct entities labeled with wrong types. Addressable by separating confusable types into different batches.\n\nThese channels are orthogonal and independently addressable. SDGD targets Channel 2; batch size reduction targets Channel 1; combining both yields additive gains.\n\n## Evidence Hierarchy (D008 — restructured after type frequency confound discovery)\n\n**Layer 1 (Primary — strongest)**: Pairwise BCS-confusion analysis\n- Pearson r=0.156 (p=4.6e-12, n=1953 type pairs); nonzero-only Spearman ρ=0.254 (p=1.1e-6)\n- Top confused pairs are all high-BCS semantic neighbors (e.g., person-other ↔ person-politician, BCS=0.934)\n- This analysis is immune to type frequency confound (operates at type-pair level)\n\n**Layer 2 (Supporting — pending)**: Per-type F1 vs mean BCS analysis\n- Controls for type frequency by computing F1 per type, not per batch\n- Partial correlation controlling for type frequency\n- Status: analysis in progress\n\n**Layer 3 (Discussion — transparent reporting)**: Batch-level BCS-F1 positive correlation\n- Within-count batch-level r(F1, BCS) is POSITIVE (high BCS → high F1), opposite to naive prediction\n- Explanation: type frequency confound — high-frequency types tend to have high BCS AND high F1\n- This is NOT evidence for the hypothesis; reported transparently as methodological finding\n- Demonstrates the importance of controlling for confounds — strengthens methodological rigor\n\n**Methodological note**: The positive batch-level correlation motivated our shift from batch-level to type-pair-level and per-type analysis. This progression (naive → controlled → mechanistic) is itself a methodological contribution.\n\n## Venue Fit Risks\n\n- **\"Just an empirical study\"**: Mitigated by SDGD as direct operationalization. Controlled experimental design parallels \"Lost in the Middle\" (TACL 2024) methodology.\n- **Training-free IE relevance**: Cite deployment scenarios -- clinical (mCODEGPT), regulatory (FINRA 2026), multilingual (50+ languages with no IE corpora). Training-free is sometimes the ONLY option.\n- **Multi-model generalizability**: MVP used only Qwen3-32B. Need >=2 models. Plan: add one model from different family.\n- **Over-prediction confound**: F1 curve reversal at low counts needs careful framing. Present recall as clean degradation signal; precision artifact explained in methodology section.\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns\n\n| Concern | Response Strategy | Experiment |\n|---------|------------------|------------|\n| Only one model | Add >=1 more model | exp-012 |\n| Only one dataset for Claim 1 | Replicate on MAVEN (168 event types, cross-task) + optionally DocRED | exp-004, exp-005 |\n| No practical method | SDGD framework | exp-008 to exp-011 |\n| Confusability measure validity | SCS validation: embedding vs empirical | exp-015 |\n| Effect size small | Report batch-level + type-pair-level + error decomposition | exp-006, exp-007 |\n| Why not just per-type splitting? | Ablation: SDGD vs per-type vs random | exp-013, exp-014 |\n| Latency overhead of decomposition | Report API calls and wall-clock time | exp-008 |\n| Type frequency confound | Transparent reporting: batch-level positive correlation + per-type and pairwise analysis controlling for frequency | exp-002, per-type analysis |\n| BCS direction unexpected | Pairwise analysis (immune to frequency) confirms hypothesis; batch-level reported in discussion | exp-002 |\n","numLines":77,"startLine":1,"totalLines":77}}}
